<article article-type="research-article" xml:lang="en" dtd-version="1.3">
  <front>
    <article-meta>
      <title-group>
        <article-title>Opt-in Enscribe markup in data-table cells (#21)</article-title>
      </title-group>
    </article-meta>
  </front>
  <body>
    <sec>
      <title>A parsed prose column</title>
      <p>Data-format tables hold data, so cells are literal by default. A <monospace>parse-columns</monospace>
attribute opts a single prose column into Enscribe inline markup while the data
columns stay literal. Long form is used (the cell content carries <monospace>&gt;</monospace>, which
would end pipe content early). The notes column links, emphasises, cross-refs the
figure below, and cites — all parsed; <monospace>sample</monospace> and <monospace>value</monospace> stay literal.</p>
      <fig id="fig:plot">
        <label>1</label>
        <caption>
          <p>A reference plot, cross-referenced from a table cell.</p>
        </caption>
        <graphic xlink:href="plot.png"/>
      </fig>
      <table-wrap id="tab:results">
        <label>1</label>
        <caption>
          <p>Results — only the notes column parses.</p>
        </caption>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>sample</th>
              <th>value</th>
              <th>notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>A</td>
              <td>3.14</td>
              <td>see <italic>the docs</italic> at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://example.org">the site</ext-link></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>B</td>
              <td>2.72</td>
              <td>compare with <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig:plot">figure 1</xref> and <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref-smith2020">smith2020</xref></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>C</td>
              <td>1.41</td>
              <td>uses <monospace>inline code</monospace> and inline math <inline-formula><tex-math><![CDATA[x^2]]></tex-math></inline-formula></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </table-wrap>
    </sec>
    <sec>
      <title>Parsing every cell</title>
      <p>With <monospace>+parse-text</monospace>, every cell parses — the all-cells opt-in.</p>
      <table-wrap>
        <label>2</label>
        <caption>
          <p>Every cell parsed.</p>
        </caption>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>label</th>
              <th>detail</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><italic>emphasised label</italic></td>
              <td>a link to <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://example.org">the site</ext-link></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </table-wrap>
    </sec>
    <sec>
      <title>Explicit opt-out</title>
      <p><monospace>-parse-text</monospace> keeps a table literal even if a document-wide default turned
parsing on. Here it is literal (no global default set), demonstrating the
attribute is accepted and leaves cells untouched.</p>
      <table-wrap>
        <label>3</label>
        <caption>
          <p>Explicitly literal.</p>
        </caption>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>token</th>
              <th>note</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>x</td>
              <td>this *stays* literal and &lt;a https://example.org | this&gt; is not a link</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </table-wrap>
    </sec>
